  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-finite-metric-coupling": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a finite nonempty set with this fixed ordering, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_j)_{j=1}^m$ be probability vectors on $X$: their coordinates are nonnegative real numbers and $\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i=\\sum_{j=1}^m q_j=1$. A **coupling** of $p$ and $q$ is a real matrix $\\pi=(\\pi_{ij})_{i,j=1}^m$ satisfying\n$$\n\\pi_{ij}\\ge 0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=p_i\\quad(1\\le i\\le m),\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=q_j\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n$$\nWrite $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. The first index records the source point and the second index records the destination point."
    ],
    "lem-finitely-generated-cone-is-closed": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $r,N\\ge1$ be integers and let $g_1,\\ldots,g_N\\in\\mathbb R^r$. Define\n$$\nC=\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^N\\lambda_i g_i:\\lambda_i\\ge0\\text{ for every }i\\right\\}.\n$$\nThen $C$ is closed in the Euclidean topology, and for all $x,y\\in C$ and all real $\\alpha,\\beta\\ge0$, one has $\\alpha x+\\beta y\\in C$."
    ],
    "def-finite-metric-wasserstein-one": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set and let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$. Let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ be probability vectors, so their coordinates are nonnegative and each vector sums to $1$. A coupling is an $m\\times m$ matrix $\\pi$ with $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$, row sums $\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}=p_i$, and column sums $\\sum_i\\pi_{ij}=q_j$; write $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. Writing $d_{ij}=d(x_i,x_j)$, define the finite-metric 1-Wasserstein transport cost by\n$$\nW_1(p,q):=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d_{ij}\\pi_{ij}.\n$$"
    ],
    "lem-finite-metric-transport-optima-attained": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite metric space, and let $p,q$ be probability vectors on $X$. The coupling set $\\Gamma(p,q)$ is nonempty and compact in $\\mathbb R^{m^2}$, so the finite-metric transport cost $W_1(p,q)$ attains its minimum. The normalized 1-Lipschitz potential set\n$$\n\\mathcal L_1(X,d)=\\{f\\in\\mathbb R^m:f_1=0,\\ |f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)\\text{ for all }i,j\\}\n$$\nis also nonempty and compact, so the linear functional $f\\mapsto\\sum_{i=1}^m(p_i-q_i)f_i$ attains a maximum on it."
    ],
    "thm-finite-metric-transport-duality": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set, let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$, $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ have nonnegative coordinates with each vector summing to $1$. Let $\\Gamma(p,q)$ consist of matrices $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$ with row sums $p_i$ and column sums $q_j$, and let\n$$\nW_1(p,q)=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d(x_i,x_j)\\pi_{ij}.\n$$\nThen\n$$\nW_1(p,q)=\\max_{\\substack{f\\in\\mathbb R^m,\\ f_1=0\\\\ |f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)\\ \\text{for all }i,j}}\\sum_{i=1}^m(p_i-q_i)f_i.\n$$\nBoth extrema are attained. Moreover, for every minimizing coupling $\\pi$ and every maximizing vector $f$, $\\pi_{ij}>0$ implies $f_i-f_j=d(x_i,x_j)$."
    ],
